# ═══ WHAT THE SERVER NEEDS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Copy to .env for local work. In production these are set on the service, and
# the three secrets are DIFFERENT VALUES on every environment — staging and
# production must not be able to mint each other's tokens.
#
# Generate one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── the database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Either DATABASE_URL, or the PG* set. The URL must be the OWNER connection:
# it runs migrations and provisions outlet roles. Request handling never uses
# it — every request runs as that outlet's own restricted login.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/kashikeyo
# PGHOST=127.0.0.1
# PGPORT=5432
# PGUSER=postgres
# PGPASSWORD=
# PGDATABASE=kashikeyo

# ── the three secrets, which are three on purpose ──────────────────────────
# A leak of one must not mint the others. In particular a stolen session secret
# can forge a session but cannot open a database connection, because every
# outlet's database password is DERIVED from OUTLET_ROLE_SECRET and stored
# nowhere.
#
# Rotating OUTLET_ROLE_SECRET changes every outlet's password: run
# `npm run provision:outlet -- --all` after, or no outlet can be reached.
OUTLET_ROLE_SECRET=change-me-at-least-32-characters-long
SESSION_SECRET=change-me-at-least-32-characters-long
PORTAL_SECRET=change-me-at-least-32-characters-long

# ── the service ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080
NODE_ENV=production
# Comma-separated. "*" is for local work only — in production name the origins.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ── the account plane (KashikeyoPOS.com) ───────────────────────────────────
# An account signs up with an email address and owns the business. Staff still
# sign in at the till with a PIN — these are different credentials for
# different people.

# Email. Without these, a sign-in code is written to the audit trail instead of
# being sent, and the app SAYS so rather than pretending it went out.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=KashikeyoPOS <no-reply@kashikeyopos.com>

# Social sign-in. A provider with no credentials is simply not offered — the
# button does not appear rather than appearing and failing. /api/account/providers
# reports which are on, and which variable NAMES a half-configured one is missing.
# Callback URLs, which must match the provider's console byte for byte:
#   https://<your domain>/api/account/oauth/google/callback
#   https://<your domain>/api/account/oauth/apple/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Apple. APPLE_CLIENT_ID is the SERVICES id (com.example.app.web), never the
# App ID — the App ID is rejected by the web flow.
#
# Apple's "client secret" is not a secret: it is a JWT signed ES256 with the .p8
# below, and Apple caps its life at six months. src/apple.js mints it and
# re-mints it before it expires, so there is nothing to diarise. Paste the whole
# .p8 including the BEGIN/END lines — newlines mangled by a dashboard are
# repaired. You can download that file from Apple exactly once.
#
# Apple also verifies the domain: put its
# apple-developer-domain-association.txt into app/well-known/ and it answers at
# /.well-known/ .
APPLE_CLIENT_ID=
APPLE_TEAM_ID=
APPLE_KEY_ID=
APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Only if you already hold a pre-minted secret; overrides the minting above and
# its expiry becomes yours to remember.
# APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Where the OAuth callback comes back to. Set this in production; locally the
# request's own host is used.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://kashikeyopos.com

# ── every store's own address ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# A store's public face is a subdomain: https://<handle>.kashikeyopos.com is
# its QR ordering portal, and /member on the same address is the customer card.
# This is the ONLY place the domain is written down — no page spells it, because
# a hostname typed into a page is right in production and wrong in staging, and
# a QR card is laminated onto forty tables before anybody scans one.
#
# Falls back to the host of PUBLIC_URL, so a normal deploy sets one variable.
# Setting it EMPTY turns store subdomains off deliberately, which is not the
# same as leaving it unset — an environment whose apex has no wildcard record
# would otherwise inherit that apex and hand out addresses that cannot resolve.
# Either way, off means every store link falls back to its path form
# (/g/<handle>), which is followable where a dead hostname is not.
#
# The DNS record and the certificate must cover *.<domain> as well as the apex.
# PORTAL_BASE_DOMAIN=kashikeyopos.com

# ── who may claim this install ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The three onboarding steps before there is anybody to sign in as — company,
# first outlet, first owner — cannot be behind a staff session, because the
# staff session is what step 3 creates. chain.claim_first_owner() succeeds
# exactly ONCE in the life of an installation, so with nothing here, whoever
# POSTs first owns the business — and a new install's hostname is in the
# certificate transparency logs minutes after its first TLS handshake.
#
# Set this and those three steps require it as x-claim-token; the panel asks
# for it once, up front. Generate one per install and hand it over with the
# address, and record it in Mission Control so you can read it back:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(12).toString('base64url'))"
#
# UNSET, they stay open — an install onboarding itself on a counter has no
# seller to get a code from — and the boot log says which of the two this
# install is, by name. It stops mattering once an owner exists.
# ONBOARDING_CLAIM_TOKEN=

# ── backups ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS INSTALL TAKES NO COPIES UNTIL ONE OF THESE IS SET, and it says so at
# boot and on the Settings card rather than implying otherwise. The platform's
# own volume snapshot is still the right thing for the CLUSTER; what this adds
# is a logical archive PER BUSINESS, so one customer can be restored without
# restoring every customer.
#
# Pick ONE destination. A directory is only a backup if it is on something that
# outlives the container — a mounted volume, not the ephemeral disk a deploy
# throws away.
# BACKUP_DIR=/var/lib/kashikeyo/backups
#
# Or any S3-compatible bucket: AWS, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO. Signed
# here with node's own crypto, so no dependency is added for it. Leave
# BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT empty for AWS itself; set it (with the account URL) for
# everything else, which is path-style.
# BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_S3_KEY=
# BACKUP_S3_SECRET=
# BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=kashikeyo
#
# How often the in-process schedule runs; 0 turns the schedule off and leaves
# `npm run backup` working. How long archives are kept — the newest good copy
# of a database is never removed by age, however old, because a business
# nobody has touched for a year still needs its last one. And how stale the
# newest good copy may get before the watchdog says so (default: twice the
# interval).
# BACKUP_EVERY_HOURS=24
# BACKUP_RETAIN_DAYS=30
# BACKUP_STALE_HOURS=48
#
# Where pg_dump and pg_restore are, if they are not on PATH. The image installs
# them; this is for a host that keeps them somewhere else. The client's major
# must be at least the server's — pg_dump refuses a server newer than itself,
# and src/backup.js refuses BY NAME rather than letting that surface at 3 a.m.
# PG_BIN_DIR=/usr/lib/postgresql/18/bin

# ── optional ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Skip the boot migration (a read replica, or a container that must not write).
# SKIP_MIGRATE=1
# Return a member's sign-in code in the API response instead of only putting it
# on the floor board. DEVELOPMENT ONLY — it turns a phone number into a login.
# MEMBER_CODE_ECHO=1
# Return an ACCOUNT sign-in code in the API response. DEVELOPMENT ONLY — it
# turns an email address into a login.
# ACCOUNT_CODE_ECHO=1
